WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
Writing instructions that change behavior.

2
00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:08.749
Most instruction files fail not because they’re missing a rule but because their rules can’t be acted on.

3
00:00:09.029 --> 00:00:28.059
The memory docs’ examples are the model to copy: “Use 2-space indentation” instead of “Format code properly”; “Run npm test before committing” instead of “Test your changes”; “API handlers live in src/api/handlers/ ” instead of “Keep files organized.

4
00:00:28.339 --> 00:00:30.493
” Each rewrite is something you could check.

5
00:00:30.773 --> 00:00:34.336
Four more principles from the docs: Specific over emphatic.

6
00:00:34.616 --> 00:00:46.776
The best-practices page lists “add IMPORTANT or ALL CAPS” under advice you can stop following: current models follow a plainly worded line, and emphasis mostly makes them over-apply the rule.

7
00:00:47.056 --> 00:00:53.200
If a line keeps getting missed, either the file is too long or the rule can’t bend and belongs in a hook.

8
00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:54.440
Consistent.

9
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.755
If two instructions contradict each other, the docs say Claude may pick one arbitrarily.

10
00:01:00.035 --> 00:01:07.459
Review nested files and rules together; /doctor prompt-audit looks for stale, contradictory, or dead references.

11
00:01:07.739 --> 00:01:09.851
Explain the why when it isn’t obvious.

12
00:01:10.131 --> 00:01:17.491
“Don’t use round() for money: it rounds half to even and our rule is half up” lets the agent handle the case you didn’t foresee.

13
00:01:17.771 --> 00:01:24.235
That’s one reason the /doctor checkup keeps pitfalls and rationale while trimming what the code already shows.

14
00:01:24.515 --> 00:01:25.752
Test the instruction.

15
00:01:26.032 --> 00:01:32.325
Treat a new line like a code change: start a fresh session, give a task where the line should matter, and watch.

16
00:01:32.605 --> 00:01:36.723
If behavior doesn’t change, the line is dead weight or wrongly worded.
